WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
Small and sharp, or few and capable?

2
00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:06.083
Here the sources pull in two directions, and it is worth seeing both.

3
00:00:06.363 --> 00:00:12.144
Anthropic’s guidance favors fewer, more capable tools over a thin wrapper per API endpoint.

4
00:00:12.424 --> 00:00:21.235
The engineering post’s example: instead of list_contacts, which makes an agent read every contact token by token, offer search_contacts.

5
00:00:21.515 --> 00:00:28.533
Instead of list_users, list_events, and create_event, consider one schedule_event that finds availability and books.

6
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:36.941
The tool docs go further and suggest grouping related operations under one tool with an action parameter, to reduce selection ambiguity.

7
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:42.299
Too many overlapping tools, the post notes, distract agents from efficient strategies.

8
00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:46.696
The security literature favors giving the agent only what it needs.

9
00:00:46.976 --> 00:01:02.165
OWASP’s entry on Excessive Agency lists “excessive functionality” as a root cause, with examples like a document-reading extension that can also delete documents, or a tool meant to run one shell command that fails to block others.

10
00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:09.784
This course’s rule of thumb reconciles them: consolidate by workflow, but never mix risk levels inside one tool.

11
00:01:10.064 --> 00:01:14.245
A search_contacts tool that only reads is a fine consolidation.

12
00:01:14.525 --> 00:01:25.939
A manage_tasks tool whose action can be list, update, or delete hides a destructive operation behind a parameter, so your gate has to inspect arguments to know what it is approving.

13
00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:32.107
Split it so read and write tools are separate, and any tool’s name tells you whether it can change the world.

14
00:01:32.387 --> 00:01:39.555
A run_sql tool that accepts arbitrary queries is the extreme case: you cannot gate what you cannot classify.
